Plan cancellation compatibility follow-up, after the first isolation seam was
integrated: the test now runs two real Assemblies concurrently with the actual
builtin `write` tool and checks both that A's write produces `tool_canceled`
with no file side effect and that B's write executes. The prior per-Assembly
wrapper-only behavior was RED because A's write reached the executor wrapper
and became a `tool_result` error instead of a canceled-call event (executor
side effects remained zero); B's write succeeded.

  rtk proxy go test ./internal/cli -list '^TestAssemblyInterceptorsAreIsolated$'
  exit=0; TestAssemblyInterceptorsAreIsolated

  rtk env GOMAXPROCS=2 go test -p 2 -count=1 ./internal/cli -run '^TestAssemblyInterceptorsAreIsolated$'
  exit=1: assembly A's builtin `write` was blocked only by the executor wrapper;
  its event list contained no `tool_canceled` call ID, while assembly B wrote
  `allowed.txt` successfully.

After composing Plan Mode then optional shell-hook interceptors on the
Assembly's LoopAgent (retaining the executor wrapper), the concurrent
integration test passed under race detection:

  rtk env GOMAXPROCS=2 go test -race -p 2 -count=1 ./internal/cli -run '^TestAssemblyInterceptorsAreIsolated$'
  exit=0

